\title{Binius}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-18}

\p{
    A proof system built from the ground up on [binary tower fields](dhsorens-002L), with [ring
    switching](dhsorens-0035) as the mechanism that keeps small values cheap. The most complete
    demonstration of the small-field argument taken to its limit.
}

\p{
    Questions this page should answer:
    \ul{
        \li{The stack: tower fields, multilinear polynomials, a small-field commitment, and
        sumcheck-based constraint checking.}
        \li{How a bit-level computation avoids paying for embedding into a large field.}
        \li{Flock, which specialises the same idea to large batches of one Boolean circuit and is
        what [made conventional hashes cheap enough to prove](dhsorens-004H) that Ethereum dropped
        Poseidon.}
        \li{Where the extension field is still required, and what fraction of the work it accounts
        for.}
        \li{The measured comparison against 31-bit prime systems, and on which workloads it holds.}
        \li{ArkLib's \code{ProofSystem/Binius} and \code{RingSwitching}: what is stated and what is
        proved.}
    }
}

\p{
    Sources: [Binary tower
    fields](https://www.irreducible.com/posts/binary-tower-fields-are-the-future-of-verifiable-computing),
    [SNARKs on binary fields](https://blog.lambdaclass.com/snarks-on-binary-fields-binius/),
    [ArkLib](https://github.com/Verified-zkEVM/ArkLib).
}
